---
/**
 * „How we measure" (audit 22. 11. 2026, E-E-A-T). Odkud jsou čísla na webu,
 * jak často se obnovují a co znamenají — odkazuje se z patičky, z About
 * a z každého průvodce. Každé tvrzení tu odpovídá kódu; kdo mění cron, build
 * nebo pravidla stránek modelek, ať upraví i tenhle text.
 */
import TextPage from '../../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import { FACETS, NETWORK_LABELS } from '../../lib/data';
import { SITE_NAME } from '../../lib/site';
---
<TextPage
	title={`How We Measure – Data, Sources & Editorial Policy | ${SITE_NAME}`}
	description="Where the numbers on eCamx come from: a live check of every cam site every five minutes, our own viewing history per model, and prices only with two sources."
	heading="How we measure"
	updated="22 November 2026"
>
	<p>
		Every number on eCamx comes from one of three places: the public live feeds of
		the cam sites, our own log of what those feeds showed over time, or a source we
		name next to the number. This page explains each one, so you can judge how far
		to trust it.
	</p>

	<h2>The live check, every five minutes</h2>
	<p>
		A scheduled job fetches the public live feed of every site we cover —
		{' '}{FACETS.networks.map((n) => NETWORK_LABELS[n.slug]).join(', ')} — every five
		minutes and stores one snapshot: who is live, in which kind of show, and, where
		the site publishes it, how many people are watching. The live grids, the
		“Live now” badges and the <a href="/status/">site status pages</a> all read that
		snapshot.
	</p>
	<p>
		If a site’s feed does not answer, we say that we could not reach it. We never
		turn a failed check into “the site is down”, and a grid keeps the last known
		cards rather than going blank.
	</p>

	<h2>The pages, rebuilt every day</h2>
	<p>
		The rest of the site — categories, countries, site reviews and model pages — is
		rebuilt from the feeds once a day. Counts in titles are rounded down
		(“2,500+”) because the exact number changes by the minute.
	</p>

	<h2>Model pages and their history</h2>
	<p>
		A model page describes what we observed, not what the performer told us. “First
		seen”, the number of days live, the largest audience and the usual streaming
		hours come from our own log. Usual hours appear only after at least a week of
		observations; before that we say nothing rather than guess.
	</p>
	<p>
		We keep pages for performers people search for and for regulars. When a
		performer has not been live for a long time, the page points to similar cams
		that are live. Performers can remove their page themselves — see
		{' '}<a href="/for-models/">for cam models</a>.
	</p>
	<p>
		The same nickname on two sites is a match of names and public profile fields
		only. We never claim it is the same person.
	</p>

	<h2>Prices and guides</h2>
	<p>
		A price gets a number only when two independent sources agree, and it is
		re-checked at least every 90 days; otherwise we show a range or nothing. The
		date of the last check is printed next to it.
	</p>
	<p>
		<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> use the live numbers above, dated snapshots of our
		own data (“on 18 November 2026 at 02:00 UTC…”) or a named source. Anything we
		could not verify is left out. Photos come from Pexels with the photographer
		credited; they illustrate the topic and never show a performer.
	</p>

	<h2>How we make money — and what it does not change</h2>
	<p>
		Links out to the cam sites carry an affiliate code, so we earn a commission
		when someone signs up or buys tokens. Grids give more room to the sites our
		visitors open most often. Commission never decides what a review says about a
		site, and the word “free” is used only for sites where watching really is free.
	</p>

	<h2>Corrections</h2>
	<p>
		Found a wrong number or an outdated claim? Write to
		{' '}<a href="mailto:info@ecamx.com">info@ecamx.com</a> and we will correct it.
	</p>
</TextPage>
